! swap_scope.strap -- SWAP, what it exchanges and what it still
! refuses.  Reference Manual printed p.30, "Swap (SWAP)":
!
!   "The number of full words specified by the count is transmitted
!    from the area which starts at the location addressed by bits
!    0-17 of the left effective address to the area which starts at
!    the location specified by bits 0-17 of the right effective
!    address.  AT THE SAME TIME, the full words of the area which
!    starts at the right effective address are transmitted to the
!    area which starts at the left effective address."
!
! >>> "AT THE SAME TIME" IS THE WHOLE INSTRUCTION, and block 4 is the
! only block that can see the ORDER the words are exchanged in. <<<
!
! Blocks 1 and 3 are one word; block 2 is three DISJOINT words, and
! disjoint words can be exchanged in any order with the same result.
! Block 4 overlaps the two areas by one word -- the case p.30's own
! PROGRAMMING NOTES single out for TRANSMIT ("when the receiving area
! overlaps the upper end of the sending area...") -- and there the
! order shows: forward gives 2 3 1 and backward gives 3 1 2.  That
! was measured, not argued: an injection that starts the swap at the
! last word and steps down passes every other block and fails only
! this one, with exactly 3 1 2.
!
! IBM'S OWN DIAGNOSTIC IS THE SHAPE OF BLOCK 1.  jb_ax4b routine 1246
! TEST 2C -- transcribed in decks/ax4b_1246_transmit_swap.strap, and
! until now checked only for its ENCODING -- zeroes the sending word,
! puts a pattern in the receiving word, swaps one word, and fails
! with
!
!     -SWAP FAILS TO ALTER ORIGINAL WD
!     -AND THEREFORE ACTS LIKE TRANSMIT
!
! if the sending word is still zero afterwards.  Block 1 is that test
! with the branch logic replaced by a storage check, because this
! harness can read storage and the diagnostic could not.
!
! WEAKER STANDARD: hand-written from the manual.  What raises it above
! the usual manual-derived deck is that the EXPECTED VALUES were
! computed from p.30's sentence by hand before the model was built,
! and block 1 reproduces an IBM test that states its own failure
! message.
!
! WHAT IS STILL REFUSED: BACKWARD swap, block 5.  SWAPB and SWAPBI are
! CARD-ONLY -- named on pocket card X22-6533 and written by no deck in
! this corpus (tests/verify_ax4b_1246_modifiers_listing.py says so) --
! so building them would be manual-derived with nothing to check
! against.  rl_xmit's sign handling would carry them unchanged.

        SLC, (8)4200.0

! ---- 1. ONE WORD, IMMEDIATE COUNT -- IBM TEST 2C's shape ----------
! A1 is zero and B1 is a pattern; after the swap A1 holds the pattern
! and B1 is zero.  A model that "acts like transmit" leaves A1 zero.
START   SWAPI, 1, A1, B1

! ---- 2. THREE WORDS, IMMEDIATE COUNT ------------------------------
! Every word of both areas is checked, so a model that exchanges the
! first word and stops fails here.
        SWAPI, 3, A2, B2

! ---- 3. ONE WORD, DIRECT COUNT ------------------------------------
! The count comes from index 1's count field, which block 3 loads
! first.  p.30: "the field J, bits 51-54, specifies an index
! register.  The count field, bits 28-45, of the index word in this
! register is used as a full word count."
        LX,   $1, CNT1
        SWAP, 1, A3, B3

! ---- 4. THE OVERLAP, which is the one that discriminates ----------
! OV, OV+1 and OV+2 hold 1, 2 and 3.  The swap exchanges (OV, OV+1)
! and then (OV+1, OV+2):
!
!   start            1  2  3
!   after word 0     2  1  3
!   after word 1     2  3  1
!
! A model that wrote the receiving word before reading it would get
! 1 1 1 for the first pair and never see the 2 at all.
        SWAPI, 2, OV, OV+1.0

! ---- 5. BACKWARD SWAP IS STILL REFUSED ----------------------------
        SWAPB, 1, A5, B5
        BZOP,  $                    ! hang if OP did NOT come on
        BOPZ,  $+.32                ! clear OP and fall through
HALT    BEW,   $

! ---- the data ------------------------------------------------------
A1      DD(BU,64,8), 0
B1      DD(BU,64,8), (8)1234567012345670123456

A2      DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000000000000000000011
        DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000000000000000000022
        DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000000000000000000033
B2      DD(BU,64,8), (8)7777777777777777777744
        DD(BU,64,8), (8)7777777777777777777755
        DD(BU,64,8), (8)7777777777777777777766

A3      DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000000000000000000077
B3      DD(BU,64,8), (8)1111111111111111111111

OV      DD(BU,64,8), 1
        DD(BU,64,8), 2
        DD(BU,64,8), 3

A5      DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000000000000000000005
B5      DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000000000000000000006

! The count register for block 3: one full word in bits 28-45.
CNT1    XW, 0, 1, 0

        END, START
